          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Let me analyze. The task: review override precedence for alternate words through dotenv_values and load_dotenv.\n\nThe requirement: preserving override precedence between earlier file values and the process environment.\n\nThe reference resolve_variables: for override=True, env.update(os.environ) then env.update(new_values) — new_values (earlier file values) win. For override=False, new_values then os.environ — environment wins. That matches the grader's expectations:\n\n- load_dotenv override=False, external='', local='file' → env wins → BASE='' → OUT='' ✓\n- override=True, external='', local='file' → file wins → 'file' → 'chosen' ✓\n- override=False, external='env', local='' → env wins → 'env' → 'chosen' ✓\n- override=True, external='env', local='' → file wins → '' → '' ✓\n\nGrader assertion: case 'precedence' — check whether assertions contradict requirement. The requirement says \"preserving override precedence between earlier file values and the process environment.\" In standard python-dotenv, override=True means file values override environment. So grader matches.\n\nvalues-precedence: dotenv_values — python-dotenv's dotenv_values ignores os.environ? Actually in real python-dotenv, dotenv_values does interpolation against os.environ too? Let me recall: resolve_variables is called with values parsed from the file only, but env includes os.environ. In real python-dotenv, dotenv_values calls resolve_variables(values, override=override) and yes, interpolation uses os.environ. Real behavior: in real python-dotenv, `env.update(os.environ)` always happens... Let me recall actual code:\n\n```python\ndef resolve_variables(values, override):\n    new_values = {}\n    for name, value in values:\n        if value is None:\n            result = None\n        else:\n            atoms = parse_variables(value)\n            env = {}\n            if override:\n                env.update(os.environ)\n                env.update(new_values)\n            else:\n                env.update(new_values)\n                env.update(os.environ)\n            result = \"\".join(atom.resolve(env) for atom in atoms)\n        new_values[name] = result\n    return new_values\n```\n\nThat's exactly the upstream code. So the reference matches upstream.\n\nGrader's values-precedence: dotenv_values(stream='BASE=file\\nOUT=${BASE:+chosen}\\n') with external='' → expected 'chosen'. Since override defaults False in dotenv_values? Actually dotenv_values has override parameter default False. So env: new_values then os.environ → os.environ wins → BASE='' → OUT=''? But grader expects 'chosen'! Wait: external='' means os.environ['BASE'] = ''. With override=False, env = {BASE:'file'} then update(os.environ) → BASE='' → ${BASE:+chosen} → '' — but grader expects 'chosen'.\n\nHmm wait — but resolve happens when parsing OUT line. At that point new_values = {BASE:'file'}. os.environ has BASE=''. override=False → env.update(new_values) → {BASE:'file'}; env.update(os.environ) → BASE=''. So OUT = ''. But grader expects 'chosen' for ('', 'file', 'chosen').\n\nWait, let me re-read grader values-precedence: `for external, local, expected in [('', 'file', 'chosen'), ('env', '', '')]`. So external='', local='file' → expected 'chosen'. That means dotenv_values should prefer file values over os.environ?? But for case 'precedence' with load_dotenv override=False, external='' local='file' → expected '' — env wins. Hmm, that's contradictory with the reference implementation which treats dotenv_values and load_dotenv the same way (both call resolve_variables with the override flag; dotenv_values override default False).\n\nWait, dotenv_values in upstream:\n\n```python\ndef dotenv_values(dotenv_path=None, stream=None, verbose=False, interpolate=True, encoding=\"utf-8\"):\n    ...\n    if dotenv_path is None and stream is None:\n        ...\n    values = parse(stream...)\n    return dict(resolve_variables(values, override=True))\n```\n\nYes! Upstream dotenv_values calls resolve_variables with override=True. Let me verify: In python-dotenv main.py:\n\n```python\ndef dotenv_values(...):\n    ...\n    return dict(resolve_variables(values, override=True))\n```\n\nYes, that's right — dotenv_values hardcodes override=True. And load_dotenv uses self.override.\n\nSo with override=True for dotenv_values: env.update(os.environ) then env.update(new_values) → file wins → BASE='file' → 'chosen' ✓ matches grader.\n\nSecond case: external='env', local='' → env.clear, BASE='env'; file has BASE='' → file wins → BASE='' → '' ✓.\n\nThe packet only shows resolve_variables, not dotenv_values. Need to check whether reference's dotenv_values passes override=True. The packet shows main.py from 10347 only. Need to look at dotenv_values in src/dotenv/main.py. Let me check with tools.\n\nAlso check resolve in variables.py: `return (self.default or \"\") if env.get(self.name) else \"\"` — env.get(self.name) truthy check: nonempty string → substitute word; empty string or None → \"\". Good.\n\nGrader 'precedence' case uses load_dotenv which sets os.environ['OUT']. With override=False and external='', local='file': env resolution — file wins? No wait: load_dotenv override=False → env.update(new_values), env.update(os.environ) → os.environ wins → BASE='' → OUT='' ✓ expected ''. override=True, external='', local='file' → new_values win → 'file' → 'chosen' ✓. external='env', local='': override=False → env wins 'env' → 'chosen' ✓; override=True → file '' wins → '' ✓. Consistent.\n\nSo the question: does the proposed reference violate, or does a grader assertion contradict? Need to check dotenv_values override in the reference. Let me read src/dotenv/main.py around dotenv_values.",
