          "text": "Good: dotenv_values uses override=True. Now check DotEnv.dict() and how override flows into resolve_variables, and set_as_environment_variables for load_dotenv. Also check the alternate resolve and how load_dotenv handles override for alternate: grader 'precedence' case expects:\n\n- override=False, external='', local='file' → OUT=''\n- override=True, external='', local='file' → 'chosen'\n- override=False, external='env', local='' → 'chosen'\n- override=True, external='env', local='' → ''\n\nWith resolve_variables: override=True → new_values win; override=False → os.environ wins. That matches.\n\nNow the `resolve` in variables.py for \"+\": `return (self.default or \"\") if env.get(self.name) else \"\"`. env.get returns the value or None. Truthy → word. Good.\n\nBut wait: does the grader contradict requirement? Grader 'precedence' case with override=True expects file value to take precedence over env for interpolation — consistent with standard load_dotenv semantics (override=True means file overrides environment). Requirement: \"preserving override precedence between earlier file values and the process environment.\" That matches.\n\nCheck also `set_as_environment_variables` — with override=False, load_dotenv won't set BASE into os.environ, but does it set OUT? Yes, OUT isn't in env. The resolution env includes os.environ regardless. Fine.\n\nNow, is there any violation in the reference? Let me check `dict()` method and parse. Also, check the alternate resolve: for `${BASE:+chosen}` when BASE is unassigned key (None value in file): grader 'disabled-and-unassigned': BASE key alone → new_values[BASE]=None → env.get('BASE') → None → '' ✓. ABSENT → env.get → None → '' ✓.\n\n'flat-braces': `${BASE:+${OTHER}}` — parse_variables with flat grammar: word ends at first closing brace, so word='${OTHER', then trailing '}' is literal. With BASE='yes' → '${OTHER' + '}' = '${OTHER}' ✓ expected. With BASE='' or None → '' + '}' = '}' ✓. That's about parsing, out of scope (nested grammar excluded anyway).\n\nFocus question: override precedence for alternate words through dotenv_values and load_dotenv. The reference matches upstream behavior and grader expectations. Does any grader assertion contradict the requirement? Requirement says \"preserving override precedence between earlier file values and the process environment\" — grader expects exactly upstream semantics. Seems consistent.\n\nLet me double check the DotEnv.dict() to make sure resolve_variables is called with self.override. Read around DotEnv class. Also verify variables.py full context of the alternate operator parse (Atom/Literal classes). Also check grader 'precedence' case 3: override=False, external='env', local='' → expected 'chosen'. load_dotenv override=False: env for resolution: new_values {BASE:''} then os.environ {BASE:'env'} → 'env' → 'chosen' ✓. But also set_as_environment_variables: BASE already in os.environ and override=False → BASE not overwritten, stays 'env'. OUT set to 'chosen' ✓.\n\nCase 4: override=True, external='env', local='' → resolution env: os.environ then new_values → BASE='' → OUT='' ✓. load_dotenv with override=True sets BASE='' into os.environ, OUT=''. Assertion only checks OUT == '' ✓.\n\nNow, one potential subtlety: the `resolve` for \"+\" uses `self.default or \"\"` — the word default; fine.\n\nLet me quickly verify DotEnv.dict passes override to resolve_variables, and view variables.py more fully to ensure the alternate branch is reached for \"${...:+...}\" (operator parsing). That's parsing though; out of scope but resolve is in scope. Let me read variables.py around offset 1769 and main.py DotEnv.dict.",
